WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
SPEAKER: Last time,
we covered how

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.750
you can use service level
indicators to measure

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.150
the reliability of
your services and how

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.420
to use service level objectives
to set reliability targets.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.880
But what metrics should you
use to accurately measure

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.170
your reliability?

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.200
Today I'm going to show you how
to use out-of-the-box platform

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.570
metrics in Google
Cloud to do just that.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.450
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.422
[MUSICAL INTRO]

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.885
Let's start by
remembering that you

00:00:26.885 --> 00:00:28.760
want to be able to
express your service level

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.940
indicators, or SLIs, as
ratios or percentages.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.120
This allows you to normalize
various measures of reliability

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.370
and set reliability
targets in a uniform way

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.260
across your services.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.140
Generally, SLIs fall
into two categories.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.660
Request-based SLIs
measure the fraction

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.760
of good units of work
against the total.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.850
For example, what percentage
of requests to an HTTP endpoint

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.490
were successful in
a given time window?

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.580
Window-based SLIs measure the
fraction of time intervals

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.340
during which the service meets
a threshold of reliability

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.720
over a given time window.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.310
Now that you know
what you're after,

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.050
which metric should you use?

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.660
Do you just start writing
custom telemetry to create them?

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.880
Now there may be scenarios
where that makes sense.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.908
And we'll cover those
later in the series.

00:01:10.908 --> 00:01:13.200
But let's start with metrics
that Google Cloud Services

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.310
create for you right
out of the box.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.210
Let's start with a
specific example.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.540
Here's a common architecture
for a web application

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.880
that uses a global load
balancer to distribute traffic

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.980
to multiple GKE clusters.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.110
For this example, you
can use these metrics

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.990
created by the global load
balancer for both availability

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.350
and latency SLIs.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.850
Let's have a look
at how you can use

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.090
service monitoring in the
Cloud operations suite

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.792
to define a service and set
an availability SLO for it.

00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:42.250
From the project dashboard,
let's go to the monitoring menu

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.083
and select services.

00:01:46.243 --> 00:01:48.160
There are a number of
services already created

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:48.882
in this project.

00:01:48.882 --> 00:01:49.840
Let's create a new one.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.000
We'll use a GKE deployment
as the basis for our service

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.082
definition.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.780
Let's name our
service and submit.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.300
Now that the service
has been created,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.310
let's define an
availability SLO for it.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.260
The first step is to set
the SLI, or service level

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.400
indicator.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.190
Services defined by App
Engine, STO, or Anthos

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:22.310
recognize availability and
latency SLIs automatically.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.480
But we've defined
a custom service.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.960
So we have to select
other as the metric.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.090
We then need to decide
whether our SLI should be

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.450
request-based or windows-based.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.200
We'll link to another
video that describes

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.250
the difference between
two in the episode notes.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.570
But for now, we'll simply
calculate a proportion of good

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.840
to total events using
a request-based SLI.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.430
The next step is to
select the metric

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:47.005
that we want to use as our SLI.

00:02:47.005 --> 00:02:49.380
Because the service is being
provided by an external load

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.860
balancer, we can use the
request count metric.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.920
We'll count all requests
in the total filter

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.720
and just those that return
a 200 in the good filter.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.490
Note that this
approach does include

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.580
potentially erroneous requests,
like those that return 404s,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:06.760
in the total filter.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.760
And you should carefully
consider whether that's

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.530
the behavior you want.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.360
Once we've made our
selections here,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.010
we can evaluate
the resulting graph

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.430
to determine whether the SLI
will be properly evaluated.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.850
Next, we'll select the
compliance period for the SLO.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.380
We want the evaluation
period to be

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.880
long enough that
we can meaningfully

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.790
evaluate and allocate error
budget toward engineering work,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.650
but not so long that
the error budget takes

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.570
too long to recover
from an incident.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.520
We also recommend
using rolling windows

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.110
for operational
concerns rather than

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.180
calendar to avoid issues with
uneven numbers of weekends,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.290
for example.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.010
If you're just getting started,
a 28-day rolling window

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.150
is great.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.310
Note that this is not
the alerting window.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.830
We'll cover alerting in
SLOs in a future episode.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.500
Now let's set a performance
goal for our SLO,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.860
evaluate the SLI against
the goal over the last hour,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.150
and click Continue to
finalize our setup.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.890
Let's name our SLO
in a way that will

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.230
allow us to easily determine
what it's measuring

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.040
and create it.

00:04:06.568 --> 00:04:08.360
Immediately, we can
see that we're actually

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.165
in the negative for error budget
for this particular service.

00:04:11.165 --> 00:04:13.040
This is because we're
using a rolling period,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.140
so the service is evaluated
over the last 28 days.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.089
This is a new service.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.010
And we simply haven't
had enough good traffic

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:22.070
to the service to recover
all of the error budget.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:26.610
This will change as our service
serves more and more traffic.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.060
So that's how you can use
service monitoring in Cloud

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.570
operations to define a custom
service and set an availability

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:37.210
SLO for it using an
out-of-the-box platform metric.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.700
If you have a request response
service that uses Cloud load

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.130
balancers, using metrics
like request count

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.280
is a great way to easily
define an availability SLI

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.530
and set a target using an SLO.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.520
But what about a data
processing pipeline?

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.290
In this example,
you want to monitor

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.240
how long it takes for
data flow to process

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.070
data elements in the pipeline.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.860
This is sometimes called
a data freshness SLI.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.650
Data flow captures this
information as two metrics.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.470
System lag measures how
long it takes the entire job

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.910
to process messages.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.280
Per stage system lag
measures how long

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.790
it takes for a given step in the
pipeline to process messages.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.908
Because our service
isn't running in a GKE,

00:05:13.908 --> 00:05:15.450
we'll have to create
a custom service

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.400
definition using the API.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.960
Make a request to the services
endpoint in your project,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.320
providing the service
ID and display name.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.530
Once the service is defined,
you can create an SLO for it.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.180
You can use the
system lag metric

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.150
that measures how long it takes
the job to process messages

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.890
as an indicator of freshness.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:36.220
Let's have a look at how to
do that in service monitoring.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.620
This time, let's start directly
on the services overview

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.400
screen.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.720
Our data pipeline service
doesn't have any SLOs.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.260
Let's create one now.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.510
Because this is a custom
service which does not

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.120
support default availability
and latency metrics,

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.480
we'll use other as
the metric type.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.540
Next, we'll select a
windows-based evaluation type.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.520
The system lag metric
measures how long

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.010
it takes data elements to
make it through our pipeline.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.380
This metric isn't
tracked per element.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.630
So we don't have a way to
count a ratio of good events

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:05.660
to total.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.350
So we'll need to use a
windows-based SLI, where we'll

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:10.870
track its average
over smaller windows

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.990
and count the ratio of those
windows that meet our threshold

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:17.280
over our evaluation period.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.850
Let's select the metric and
specify our window criteria.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.950
Based on the historical
data shown here,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:27.640
we can expect the average system
lag to be under two seconds.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.480
A five-minute window is
a great place to start.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.300
Note that shorter windows will
be more responsive to changes,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.330
but could also result
in a noisier signal.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.780
Now that we've configured our
SLI, let's define our SLO.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:46.230
We'll again use a 28-day rolling
period and set a target of 99%.

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:50.460
This means that we expect 99%
of our five-minute windows

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.480
over a 28-day period to
have an average system

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.610
lag of under two seconds.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.010
Let's give our SLO
a good name and see

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.510
how our service is performing.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.570
We can now see how
our SLI has been

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:06.940
performing against our
target and how much

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.410
error budget remains.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.870
And that's how you can set
up a data freshness SLI

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:14.460
for a streaming data pipeline.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.380
You've now seen how to
use platform metrics

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.600
to define SLIs and
SLOs for services

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.300
that use load balancers,
and for data pipelines.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.970
But what about cases where none
out-of-the-box metrics work

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.020
for you?

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.062
One option that might is
to use logs-based metrics.

00:07:28.062 --> 00:07:29.520
Let's have a look
at how to do that

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.950
using Cloud logging, Cloud
monitoring, and service

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.240
monitoring.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.880
Here's a service that emits
a log entry for every request

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:38.890
that includes a status code.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:41.710
Successful responses
include a 200 status code,

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.960
server errors include a 500
error status, and so on.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.760
From these log
entries, we can create

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.170
a logs-based metric that will
count the number of requests.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:55.290
The status label will allow us
to track successful requests

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.770
separately from failed ones.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.660
We can view the metric
in Metrics Explorer

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.180
to confirm that it's
working as we expect.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.430
We're now ready to configure
the SLI for our service

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:08.620
from this metric and
set an SLO for it.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.170
Let's go to our services
overview, select our service,

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.950
and create an SLO for it.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.320
Because we're essentially
using a custom metric,

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.940
let's select other
as the metric type.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.825
We'll create a
request-based SLI,

00:08:20.825 --> 00:08:22.200
because well want
it to calculate

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.650
a ratio of successful
requests to all requests.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:29.523
Let's select our metric
to be used as the SLI.

00:08:29.523 --> 00:08:31.190
We'll count the total
number of requests

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:33.230
by using the unfiltered metric.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:36.419
We'll count the number
of good requests

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:41.520
by only counting those
that return a 200.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:44.760
Let's have our SLO evaluated
over a rolling 28-day period

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:45.840
with a target of 95%.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.730
Now we can see how
our SLI is performing

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.630
against our availability
target and how much

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.850
error budget we have remaining
in this evaluation period.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.670
And that's how you create
an SLI and SLO using

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:07.950
logs-based metrics.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.210
Thanks for joining me today.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:10.830
In this episode,
we looked at how

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.680
to use platform metrics to
define service level indicators

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.860
and set service level
objectives against those.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.510
We covered using
load balancer metrics

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.180
for request-response services,
data flow metrics for data

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:24.120
processing pipelines, and
logs-based metrics for services

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.573
that may not emit good
SLIs out of the box.

00:09:26.573 --> 00:09:27.990
You're now ready
to start defining

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.023
SLIs for your own services.

00:09:30.023 --> 00:09:32.190
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.190
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:35.820
See you soon.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.170
[MUSICAL OUTRO]

